Two instruments that separate a lost repaint from a repaint nobody asked for.

An application that paints once and never again gives no evidence at all by itself, and the two
explanations want opposite fixes: either nothing marks the view dirty, or something marks it and
the mark is lost on the way to the window. Neither existing trace could tell them apart.

PER WINDOW, NOT GLOBALLY. cider-dirty kept ONE static timestamp for every window, so whichever
window was asked first in each interval printed and the rest were invisible. iTerm2 reported one
dirty invisible window 93 times and said nothing at all about the window actually on screen, and I
read that as the terminal window never being dirty. It is per window now, and it also prints the
key flag and the frame, which is what tells the real window from the offscreen ones.

THE WHOLE VIEW MARK WAS UNTRACED. setNeedsDisplayInRect: has named its caller for a long time;
setNeedsDisplay:, which is what a terminal uses, named nothing. It now prints the view, the flag,
the window number or -1, the frame and the visible rect. The visible rect matters because
setNeedsDisplay:YES empties the invalid rect list to mean the whole view, and the display then
draws unionOfInvalidRects, which for an empty list IS the visible rect: a view marked dirty whose
visible rect is empty is marked and redrawn to nothing, and looks exactly like a mark never made.

WHAT THEY MEASURED FOR iTerm2 3.5.14, all of it new and none of it guessable from the old traces:
  - the terminal view IS marked dirty, 482 times in one run
  - 313 of those marks arrive while the view has NO window and are silently dropped, because
    setNeedsDisplay: sends setViewsNeedDisplay: to nil
  - it is ONE view, not two: the same pointer, windowless for 313 consecutive marks and then in
    window 3 for 169
  - its visible rect is real, 1241x632, so the empty-rect failure above is NOT what is happening
  - 91 marks arrive after the keyboard does, and the window they reach is visible
Task #239 carries the chain and what is still open.

